<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madockawando</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1698">1698</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madockawando was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a> sachem in what is now Maine, whose daughter married the French officer Jean-Vincent d'Abbadie de Saint-Castin. In King William's War he led attacks on English settlements, including the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> in 1692, while also negotiating with the English, and he took part in the peace made at Pemaquid in 1693.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Madockawando was born in Maine around 1630 and was the adopted son of Assaminasqua, whom he succeeded as sachem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a>. The Penobscot lands east of the Penobscot River formed part of the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, which the English returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1667 Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, although the English claimed that the land between the Penobscot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St. Croix</a> rivers fell within the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a>'s patent. Jean-Vincent d'Abbadie de Saint-Castin, a trader who settled among the Penobscot, drew them into the French interest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the war of King Philip's confederacy with the Plymouth Colony spread to Maine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a> were the first of the Native peoples to sue for peace, and Madockawando ratified the articles subscribed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 6 November 1676. The English renewed hostilities, and the Penobscot destroyed the English settlements in that part of Maine. A treaty made at Casco in 1678 let English settlers return to their farms on condition of paying rent to the Penobscot.</p><p>The peace lasted until 1688, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a> arrived in a frigate and sacked Saint-Castin's house. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penobscot">Penobscot</a> chiefs, supplied with arms by Saint-Castin, attacked English settlements, one of the causes of King William's War, and Madockawando took a prominent part in every stage of the conflict. When the English built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-william-henry">Fort William Henry</a> at Pemaquid he hurried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> to tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-de-buade-de-frontenac">Louis de Buade de Frontenac</a>, and his messengers warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a>'s expedition. After agreeing to peace in 1693 he could not persuade the chiefs under the influence of French Jesuit emissaries and had to resume hostilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The war went on for more than a year after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Peace of Ryswick</a>. Madockawando moved to Meductic, a Maliseet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> mission village on the St. John River, where he died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic in 1698. A plaque in Castine, Maine, recorded that in 1692 an English boy taken captive at Casco and held by Madockawando was tortured and burned at the stake for trying to escape, a form of ritual torture used against enemies taken in war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madockawando” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leaders such as the Penobscot sachem <b>Madockawando</b> dealt with both empires.</blockquote><a href="/state/wabanaki-confederacy">Wabanaki Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maine, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Maine, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-69&amp;year=1688" aria-label="Maine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Madockawando"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4463 6190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4463 6190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-69&amp;year=1688">What was here in 1688? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
